{% note
   text="User and group information can reveal privileged accounts, service accounts, unusual login shells, and potential paths for further investigation.\n\nOn a compromised Linux host, start by understanding who exists and what privileges they have." /%}

### User Enumeration

{% terminal
   title="user enumeration"
   commands=[{cmd: "cat /etc/passwd"}, {cmd: "getent passwd"}, {cmd: "who"}, {cmd: "w"}] /%}

{% twoColumn
   left="Check:\n\n• Username\n• UID\n• Home directory\n• Login shell\n\nUID 0 accounts are especially important because they have root-level privileges."
   right="Check group memberships for privileged groups such as:\n\nsudo\nwheel\ndocker\n\nGroup membership can grant significant access without directly being root." /%}

{% terminal
   title="privilege enumeration"
   commands=[{cmd: "id"}, {cmd: "groups"}, {cmd: "getent group sudo"}, {cmd: "getent group wheel"}] /%}

{% finding
   severity="informational"
   text="Look for:\n\n• Unexpected UID 0 accounts\n• Users with privileged group memberships\n• Service accounts with login shells\n• Unusual home directories\n• Recently created accounts\n• Accounts with unexpected SSH access" /%}

{% warning
   text="Many system accounts are intentionally created without interactive login access.\n\nAn unusual account is an investigation lead, not automatically evidence of compromise." /%}

{% tip
   text="A service account with an interactive shell can be more interesting than one configured with a non-login shell.\n\nCompare the account's purpose with its configured shell and privileges." /%}

### Takeaway

Start Linux account enumeration with users, UIDs, groups, shells, and active sessions. Privilege and access should always be considered together.
